# The response headers Cloudflare Pages sends for this site.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS NOT SERVED, AND IT IS NOT THE FILE THAT SHIPS.
#
# Cloudflare parses `_headers` out of the build output and never serves it
# ("will not itself be served as a static asset, but will instead be parsed").
# So nothing you can request from the deployed site shows you this file; the
# only way to see what it did is to read the response headers of some OTHER
# URL, with `curl -sI`. Step F of the handover checklist in `docs/handover.md`
# gives the exact commands, against the Pages deployment rather than against
# www.bor-zh.ch, which still serves the old WordPress install.
#
# And what ships is `dist/_headers`, not this file: `script-src` below carries
# a PLACEHOLDER that `scripts/csp-hash.mjs` replaces at build time with the
# SHA-256 of every inline script Astro emitted. See the note on `script-src`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TWO RULES MUST NEVER SET THE SAME HEADER NAME. Cloudflare applies every
# matching rule and, when a header name appears twice, JOINS THE VALUES WITH A
# COMMA rather than letting the more specific rule win. Two `Cache-Control`
# rules would therefore produce `Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, no-store`,
# which is not either policy. Every header name below appears in exactly one
# rule; keep it that way. (To drop a header Cloudflare adds by default, prefix
# the name with `! ` rather than re-declaring it.)
#
# Limits: at most 100 rules, and 2,000 characters per line including the name.
# The `Content-Security-Policy` line is the one that could approach that: each
# script hash costs about 52 characters.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https://avatars.githubusercontent.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; script-src 'self' {{hash-scripturi}}; connect-src 'self' https://api.github.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), interest-cohort=()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `script-src` AND THE PLACEHOLDER. Read this before editing the line above.
#
# `script-src 'self'` alone WOULD BREAK THIS SITE, SILENTLY. Task 10's week
# picker is about 3 KB, which is under Vite's inline threshold, so Astro writes
# it INTO the homepage as `<script type="module">...</script>`. An inline script
# is not covered by `'self'`: Chrome refuses it with
#
#   Executing inline script violates the following Content Security Policy
#   directive 'script-src 'self''.
#
# and there is no visible symptom, because running without that script is the
# DESIGNED fallback - every week renders, the picker bar stays hidden, and the
# next-service card keeps whatever value the build stamped on it. The page looks
# perfect and tells a parishioner about a Liturgy that finished hours ago.
# Measured in headless Chrome over the real build, both with and without the
# hash. `npm run a11y` IS that measurement, and it still fails on the
# missing-hash case: the refusal it reports is `script-src-elem <- inline`.
#
# So the policy names the script BY HASH. `'unsafe-inline'` would also work and
# is not acceptable here: it would re-admit every injected `<script>` this
# directive exists to stop. A hash admits exactly one byte sequence, the one we
# built.
#
# The hash changes with every change to the script, which is why it cannot be
# written here by hand. `scripts/csp-hash.mjs` runs at `astro:build:done`,
# hashes every inline executable script in every built page, and substitutes
# them for `{{hash-scripturi}}`. It FAILS THE BUILD if the placeholder is
# missing, so deleting it does not quietly fall back to a policy that blocks the
# script - and `src/lib/headers.itest.ts` asserts the shipped `dist/_headers`
# carries a real hash and no placeholder.
#
# `<script type="application/json">` - the homepage's schedule island - is DATA,
# not code. CSP does not police it and it gets no hash. Verified: the only
# violation Chrome reported was for the module.
#
# `style-src` keeps `'unsafe-inline'` because `astro.config.mjs` sets
# `inlineStylesheets: 'always'`, so every page's CSS is an inline `<style>`.
# Hashing those too is possible and was not done: a stylesheet cannot execute,
# and the hashes would be several per page rather than one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# LOAD-BEARING, AND UNVERIFIABLE FROM THIS REPOSITORY. A static build discards
# the `Content-Type` the endpoint itself sets (`src/pages/program.ics.ts` sets
# it, and `astro dev` honours it; `astro build` writes only the body), so what a
# subscriber's calendar client receives is decided HERE and nowhere else. No
# test in this repository can see it. Verify against the deployed host:
#
#   curl -sI https://<project>.pages.dev/program.ics | grep -i content-type
#   -> content-type: text/calendar; charset=utf-8
#
# If it comes back `text/calendar; charset=utf-8, text/calendar` the comma-join
# above has happened and some other rule is also setting `Content-Type`.
#
# One hour of caching: the feed changes only when a volunteer publishes, and a
# calendar client polls on its own schedule anyway. Long enough to matter, short
# enough that a corrected service time is not stuck for a day.
/program.ics
  Content-Type: text/calendar; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# `/admin/` is the page where an editor's GitHub credential lives, so it gets
# the same `'self'` treatment as the rest of the site rather than an exception:
# the CMS's three fonts are vendored from npm at the exact versions its own URLs
# named, and `scripts/copy-cms.mjs` asserts each URL is found exactly once so an
# upgrade that moves them fails the build instead of silently restoring the CDN.
#
# FIVE requests are deliberately still blocked here, each measured in a real
# browser and each failing gracefully - the sign-in screen draws correctly with
# all five refused:
#
#   connect-src  data:                                    the CMS fetching its
#                                                         own logo as a data:
#                                                         SVG; console error
#                                                         only
#   connect-src  https://unpkg.com/@sveltia/cms/...       an hourly update
#                                                         check; the version is
#                                                         pinned on purpose
#   connect-src  https://www.githubstatus.com/api/...     the incident banner;
#                                                         the start-up page
#                                                         already tells a
#                                                         volunteer what they
#                                                         need
#   img-src      blob:  (x2)                              image data the CMS
#                                                         wraps in a blob URL.
#                                                         Not in the task 12
#                                                         report - found by
#                                                         measuring. If an
#                                                         editor ever reports a
#                                                         missing thumbnail in
#                                                         the media library,
#                                                         this is why, and
#                                                         `blob:` in `img-src`
#                                                         is the fix.
#
# `CSP_ASTEPTAT` in `scripts/a11y.mjs` holds that list as the EXPECTED set and
# fails when a sixth appears, so a CMS upgrade that reaches for a new origin
# cannot do it quietly. It also fails when an expected one STOPS appearing,
# which is what catches an audit that measured nothing.
#
# That name was wrong in this file until somebody checked it - it pointed at a
# script that has never existed. `src/lib/headers.itest.ts` now resolves every
# path named here, because an instruction naming a file that does not exist
# reads exactly like an instruction that works.
#
# The start-up page named in the table above is `public/admin/start.mjs`.
#
# EVERY REPOSITORY PATH IN THIS FILE IS WRITTEN IN BACKTICKS, and that is a
# chosen limit rather than an oversight. The resolver looks only inside
# backticks, because outside them there is no way to tell a path from a route
# pattern or a URL - both of which this file is full of. So the convention is
# what makes "every path here resolves" a complete claim, and the same test
# enforces it: a file-shaped token in a comment, outside backticks and outside
# a URL, fails. The table above named that start-up script bare for two rounds
# - correct, resolvable, and invisible to the guard, which is the whole reason
# the convention is now enforced rather than merely followed.
#
# UNVERIFIED until the first real sign-in, which needs credentials no agent has:
# `img-src avatars.githubusercontent.com` for the editor's avatar, and whatever
# the OAuth flow needs - the Worker origin in `connect-src`, possibly
# github.com. Watch the browser console on that first attempt; step G of
# `docs/handover.md` says what to do with each message.
/admin/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
